alled H1, H2, ..., Hk. Let x_i^j ∈ V(H^j) with 1 ≤ i ≤ 6 and 1 ≤ j ≤ k, and let x_6^j x_1^{j+1} ∈ E(G1) for each 1 ≤ j ≤ k − 1. Aside from H1 and Hk, each copy of H has exactly two vertices of degree 2, and only one of these can be included in the independent set I. Each of H1 and Hk have two independent vertices of degree 2, so |I| = n/6 + 2.\n\nSecond, construct G2 by starting with a triangle, and for each of its vertices, connect it by an edge to a new triangle. Then for each vertex of degree 2 in this graph, connect it by an edge to a new triangle. Repeat this process k times. In G2, every vertex of degree 2 is adjacent to another vertex of degree 2, so only one of each pair can be in I. By adding a triangle adjacent to each vertex of degree 2 in the pair, we can increase the size of I by 1, and we have added 6 vertices. That means the limit\n\n\\[\n\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}\\frac{|I|}{n}=\\frac{1}{6},\n\\]\n\nso no larger proportion than 1/6 of the vertices of G2 can be in I.\n\nWe also note the following easy-to-prove facts about graphs with no chorded cycles. We did not use these facts in our proof of Theorem 5 but they may be of interest to the reader.\n\n**Fact 1**  If G is a graph of order n with no chorded cycles, then there exists an ordering of the vertices of G such that each vertex has at most two neighbors preceding it in this ordering. Further G is a bipartite graph.\n\n**Fact 2**  If G is a graph of order n containing no chorded cycles, then \\(|E(G)| \\le 2n - 4\\).\n\n**Acknowledgements**  The authors would like to thank the referees for their suggestions and comments, and thank Ariel Keller Rorabaugh for the helpful insights she provided. The second author is supported by the\n\nSpringer--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConjectural combinatorial formula for the parabolic H-triangle — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/344314/conjectural-combinatorial-formula-for-the-parabolic-h-triang)\nciteturn1search0 [w