ver’s conjecture when d = 3.¹ The second part remains as a long-standing open problem.\n\nConjecture 1.1 (Maximality conjecture). The generic 3-dimensional rigidity matroid on K(V) is the unique maximal abstract 3-rigidity matroid on K(V).\n\nWhiteley [21] found another candidate for a maximal abstract d-rigidity matroid from approximation theory. Consider a plane polygonal domain D which has been subdivided into a set of polygonal faces Δ. A bivariate C^r_s-spline over Δ is a function on D which is continuously differentiable r times and is given by a polynomial of degree s over each face of Δ. The set of all C^r_s-splines over Δ forms a vector space S^r_s(Δ), and determining the dimension of S^r_s(Δ) is a major question in this area. When r = 0 and d = 1, S^0_1(Δ) is the space of piecewise linear functions, and Maxwell’s reciprocal diagram gives a concrete correspondence between S^0_1(Δ) and the self-stresses of the 1-skeleton of Δ, regarded as a 2-dimensional framework (G, p). Whiteley [19–21] extended this connection further and demonstrated how the dimension of S^r_s(Δ) can be computed from the rank of the C^r_s-cofactor matrix C^r_s(G, p), which is a variant of the incidence matrix of\n\n¹ The first part of Graver’s conjecture, that there exists a unique maximal abstract d-rigidity matroid on K(V), would follow for all d as a special case of the main result in a preprint of Sitharam and Vince [17]. Unfortunately this more general result is false, see [9, 14].\n\nDISCRETE ANALYSIS, 2022:2, 50pp. 2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRigidity of Frameworks - Lecture 2  The Maximal Abstract 3-Rigidity Matroid (https://www.fields.utoronto.ca/talk-media/1/30/89/slides.pdf)\nciteturn1search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.8 years ago; Graver’s Maximality Conjecture ... Chen, Sitharam and Vince sketched an algorithmic proof of (a) when \\(d = 3\\) (Banff, 2015).\nGraver’s Maximality Conjecture\n\nGraver observed that, if 